Chapter: Chapter Scoper
  cites Foreword chapter Sort
  cites Codex chapter Build Settings (demand-scope-guard-band)
  cites Codex chapter Phase Allocator (deck-short-of)

 Resolves name collisions across chapters by prefixing duplicate
 definition names with their chapter slug. Only colliding names
 (defined in 2+ chapters) are renamed. Non-colliding names pass
 through unchanged.

Section: Chapter Assignment
  ChapterAssignment = record {
   def-name : Text,
   chapter-slug : Text
  }

  assign-chapters : List DefHeader, Text, Integer, List ChapterAssignment -> List ChapterAssignment
  assign-chapters (headers) (current-chapter) (i) (acc) =
   if i == list-length headers then acc
   else let hdr = list-at headers i
   in assign-chapters headers current-chapter (i + 1) (list-push acc (ChapterAssignment {
    def-name = token-text (hdr.name),
    chapter-slug = current-chapter
   }))

Section: Collision Detection
  find-colliding-names : List ChapterAssignment -> SkipListText
  find-colliding-names (assignments) =
   find-collisions-loop assignments 0 (list-length assignments) skip-list-text-empty [] []

  find-collisions-loop : List ChapterAssignment, Integer, Integer, SkipListText, List Text, List Text -> SkipListText
  find-collisions-loop (assignments) (i) (len) (acc) (seen-names) (seen-slugs) =
   if i == len then acc
   else let a = list-at assignments i
   in if a.def-name == opening-entry-point then find-collisions-loop assignments (i + 1) len acc seen-names seen-slugs
      else if skip-list-text-has acc (a.def-name) then find-collisions-loop assignments (i + 1) len acc seen-names seen-slugs
      else let slen = list-length seen-names
      in let pos = bsearch-text-set seen-names (a.def-name) 0 slen
      in if pos < slen then
        if list-at seen-names pos == a.def-name then
          if list-at seen-slugs pos /= a.chapter-slug
            then find-collisions-loop assignments (i + 1) len (skip-list-text-insert acc (a.def-name)) seen-names seen-slugs
            else find-collisions-loop assignments (i + 1) len acc seen-names seen-slugs
        else find-collisions-loop assignments (i + 1) len acc (list-insert-at seen-names pos (a.def-name)) (list-insert-at seen-slugs pos (a.chapter-slug))
      else find-collisions-loop assignments (i + 1) len acc (list-insert-at seen-names pos (a.def-name)) (list-insert-at seen-slugs pos (a.chapter-slug))

  count-openings : List ChapterAssignment, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  count-openings (assignments) (i) (len) (count) =
   if i == len then count
   else let a = list-at assignments i
   in if a.def-name == opening-entry-point then count-openings assignments (i + 1) len (count + 1)
      else count-openings assignments (i + 1) len count

  check-entry-point-duplicate : List ChapterAssignment -> List Diagnostic
  check-entry-point-duplicate (assignments) =
   if count-openings assignments 0 (list-length assignments) 0 >= 2
   then [make-error cdx-duplicate-definition "'opening' is defined in multiple chapters. A program has exactly one entry point." synthetic-span]
   else []

Section: Builtin Shadowing

 Emit resolves a user definition ahead of a builtin and says nothing:
 X86_64Compound's dispatch reads `if user-arity < 0 & is-builtin ...`,
 so where a call reaches that dispatch, a definition of the same name
 wins for EVERY call site in the compilation unit, including the
 compiler's own if the chapter is cited into it. Nothing anywhere
 reported that, and nothing does yet.

 It does not win everywhere, and that is the sharper half. Measured
 2026-07-18 by shadowing five builtins in one chapter and printing what
 each call returned: abs, int-mod, text-contains and is-digit took the
 definition; text-length took the BUILTIN and ignored the definition
 entirely. A builtin lowered to its own IR node never reaches the
 dispatch a definition could displace. So the same shadowing pattern
 resolves two different ways depending on which builtin it names, and
 the source says nothing about which -- you would have to know how that
 particular builtin is lowered. Inconsistent is worse than either
 answer consistently, and it is the reason to say something at the
 definition rather than leave it to be reasoned out at each call.

 A warning, not an error, and deliberately so. Most shadows are benign
 and a few are load-bearing: CCE defines is-digit / is-letter /
 is-whitespace over CCE code points rather than ASCII, and StringUtils
 defines text-contains / text-replace / text-starts-with, and those are
 the definitions their callers want. An error would break them, and an
 error nobody can act on is a build everyone learns to bypass.

 What the warning buys is that the dangerous case stops being silent.
 The danger is not a different ANSWER, it is a different COST: `Hamt`
 defined list-insert-at -- a builtin that is in-place and amortised
 O(1) -- with a pure O(n) body, and citing Hamt into the compiler sent
 TypeEnv, ChapterScoper, TypeChecker and LambdaLifting quadratic until
 R10 walked into the stack. That shadow was correct by every test and
 wrong by every clock.

 Measured against the compiler's own unit when this shipped: 7 sites.

 The name set is built once by the caller. builtin-names is a
 zero-parameter definition, so a mention of one is a call that rebuilds
 the whole list -- the same shape that made emit-builtin expensive
 before the table moved into CodegenState.

  check-shadowed-builtins : List DefHeader, SkipListText, Integer, Integer, List Diagnostic -> List Diagnostic
  check-shadowed-builtins (headers) (bset) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let h = list-at headers i
   in let n = token-text (h.name)
   in if skip-list-text-has bset n
      then check-shadowed-builtins headers bset (i + 1) len (list-push acc (make-warning cdx-shadows-builtin ("Definition '" & n & "' has the same name as a builtin. Whether a call site gets this definition or the builtin depends on how that builtin is lowered, and the source does not say which -- so if this is deliberate, it is deliberate about something you cannot read here. If it is not, rename it. Check the COST as well as the answer: a shadow that computes the same result more slowly is the failure mode that has actually cost time here. This chapter's shadow applies to the whole compilation unit, and to any unit that cites it.") (token-span (h.name))))
      else check-shadowed-builtins headers bset (i + 1) len acc

Section: Colliding Across Chapters

 The mangling below is what makes a collision survivable: two chapters
 may each define a name and each keeps its own. What it does not do is
 say so. A mention resolves by the chapter it sits in, and in a chapter
 that defines neither the winner is the first assignment in concat
 order, which is the directory glob and not anything written in the
 source. So the same body means different things in different files,
 and moving one is silent.

 It has been paid for once already. CL 8955 moved three builtin emitter
 bodies from the Emit quire into Types, where emit-expr resolved to the
 text emitter instead of the x86 one. That was caught, but only because
 the two signatures were far enough apart to raise CDX2001. Two
 colliding definitions with compatible types compile clean and emit the
 wrong code, and nothing looks wrong anywhere.

 A warning at each definition site, not an error: a collision is legal
 and sometimes deliberate, and both halves of a parallel API are
 legitimate code. What it costs is bounded by the collision count,
 because only a name in the colliding set pays for the scan that names
 the other chapter.

 This paragraph used to end "the compiler's own unit carries none as of
 the CL that shipped this, so it is silent where it should be silent."
 That was false when it was written and stayed false: measured
 2026-07-19 by compiling the compiler source and counting, the unit
 carried two. `cce-to-unicode-table` sat in both this quire's x86-64
 emitter and `Foreword--CCE`, two 128-entry tables that were
 byte-identical and that nothing compared. `find-def-index` sat in both
 `Syntax--Parser` and `Types--Type Checker`, which is the other shape
 entirely: two different functions over two different trees that had
 merely been given one name.

 Both are gone -- the table by deletion in favour of the foreword's, the
 lookup by taking a chapter-specific prefix -- so the claim is true now.
 It is restated as history rather than removed because a doc that
 asserted its own cleanliness, and was believed, is the thing that let
 the count sit at two while a backlog entry recorded one.

  colliding-other-chapter : List ChapterAssignment, Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  colliding-other-chapter (assignments) (name) (own-slug) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then ""
   else let a = list-at assignments i
   in if a.def-name == name then if a.chapter-slug /= own-slug then a.chapter-slug
      else colliding-other-chapter assignments name own-slug (i + 1) len
      else colliding-other-chapter assignments name own-slug (i + 1) len

  check-cross-chapter-collisions : List DefHeader, SkipListText, List ChapterAssignment, Integer, Integer, List Diagnostic -> List Diagnostic
  check-cross-chapter-collisions (headers) (colliding) (assignments) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let h = list-at headers i
   in let n = token-text (h.name)
   in if skip-list-text-has colliding n
      then let other = colliding-other-chapter assignments n (h.chapter-slug) 0 (list-length assignments)
   in check-cross-chapter-collisions headers colliding assignments (i + 1) len (list-push acc (make-warning cdx-collides-across-chapters ("Definition '" & n & "' is also defined in chapter '" & other & "'. Both are kept and each chapter sees its own, so this compiles and runs -- but which definition a mention gets depends on the chapter the mention sits in, and in a chapter that defines neither it depends on the order the build globs files. Move this body to the other chapter and it silently means the other thing. Give one of them a chapter-specific prefix, or cite the one you mean instead of defining it twice.") (token-span (h.name))))
      else check-cross-chapter-collisions headers colliding assignments (i + 1) len acc

Section: Name Mangling
  mangle-name : Text, Text -> Text
  mangle-name (chapter-slug) (def-name) =
   slugify chapter-slug & "_" & def-name

  slugify-loop : Text, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  slugify-loop (s) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else let c = char-code-at s i
   in if c == char-code-at " " 0 then slugify-loop s (i + 1) len (list-push acc "-")
      else if c >= cc-first-upper then if c <= cc-upper-z then slugify-loop s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (char-to-text (code-to-char (c - 26))))
      else slugify-loop s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (char-to-text (code-to-char c)))
      else slugify-loop s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (char-to-text (code-to-char c)))

  slugify : Text -> Text
  slugify (s) = text-concat-list (slugify-loop s 0 (text-length s) [])

  lookup-chapter : List ChapterAssignment, Text -> Text
  lookup-chapter (assignments) (name) =
   lookup-chapter-loop assignments name 0 (list-length assignments)

  lookup-chapter-loop : List ChapterAssignment, Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  lookup-chapter-loop (assignments) (name) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then ""
   else let a = list-at assignments i
   in if a.def-name == name then a.chapter-slug
      else lookup-chapter-loop assignments name (i + 1) len

Section: Rename Map
  RenameEntry = record {
   original : Text,
   mangled : Text
  }

Section: Binary Search — RenameEntry (lower bound by original)
  bsearch-rename-pos : List RenameEntry, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bsearch-rename-pos (entries) (name) (lo) (hi) =
   if lo >= hi then lo
   else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
   in if text-compare name (list-at entries mid).original <= 0 then bsearch-rename-pos entries name lo mid
      else bsearch-rename-pos entries name (mid + 1) hi

  sorted-rename-insert : List RenameEntry, RenameEntry -> List RenameEntry
  sorted-rename-insert (entries) (entry) =
   list-insert-at entries (bsearch-rename-pos entries (entry.original) 0 (list-length entries)) entry

Section: Apply Rename
  scope-copy-text : Text -> Text
  scope-copy-text (t) =
   if text-length t == 0 then "" else deck-record (substring t 0 (text-length t))

  rename-lookup : List RenameEntry, Text -> Text
  rename-lookup (entries) (name) =
   let len = list-length entries
   in let pos = bsearch-rename-pos entries name 0 len
   in if pos < len then
     if (list-at entries pos).original == name then scope-copy-text ((list-at entries pos).mangled)
     else name
   else name

  is-colliding : SkipListText, Text -> Boolean
  is-colliding (names) (name) =
   skip-list-text-has names name

Section: IR Expression Renaming
  rename-has-entry : List RenameEntry, Text -> Boolean
  rename-has-entry (entries) (name) =
   let len = list-length entries
   in let pos = bsearch-rename-pos entries name 0 len
   in if pos >= len then False
      else (list-at entries pos).original == name

  remove-rename : List RenameEntry, Text -> List RenameEntry
  remove-rename (entries) (name) =
   if rename-has-entry entries name then
    remove-rename-loop entries name 0 (list-length entries) []
   else entries

  remove-rename-loop : List RenameEntry, Text, Integer, Integer, List RenameEntry -> List RenameEntry
  remove-rename-loop (entries) (name) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else let e = list-at entries i
   in if e.original == name then remove-rename-loop entries name (i + 1) len acc
      else remove-rename-loop entries name (i + 1) len (list-push acc e)

  remove-renames-for-params : List RenameEntry, List IRParam, Integer -> List RenameEntry
  remove-renames-for-params (entries) (params) (i) =
   if i == list-length params then entries
   else let p = list-at params i
   in remove-renames-for-params (remove-rename entries (p.name)) params (i + 1)

  rename-ir-expr : List RenameEntry, IRExpr -> IRExpr
  rename-ir-expr (rn) (e) =
   when e
    is IrIntLit (v) (sp) | IrNumLit (v) (sp) | IrTextLit (v) (sp) | IrBoolLit (v) (sp) | IrCharLit (v) (sp) | IrError (msg) (ty) (sp) -> e
    is IrFork (x) (ty) (sp) -> IrFork (rename-ir-expr rn x) ty sp
    is IrAwait (x) (ty) (sp) -> IrAwait (rename-ir-expr rn x) ty sp
    is IrNegate (x) (t) (sp) -> IrNegate (rename-ir-expr rn x) t sp
    is IrName (n) (ty) (sp) -> IrName (rename-lookup rn n) ty sp
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (ty) (sp) -> IrBinary op (rename-ir-expr rn l) (rename-ir-expr rn r) ty sp
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> IrApply (rename-ir-expr rn f) (rename-ir-expr rn a) ty sp
    is IrIf (c) (t) (el) (ty) (sp) -> IrIf (rename-ir-expr rn c) (rename-ir-expr rn t) (rename-ir-expr rn el) ty sp
    is IrFieldAccess (rec) (field) (ty) (sp) -> IrFieldAccess (rename-ir-expr rn rec) field ty sp
    is IrLet (nm) (ty) (val) (body) (sp) -> rename-ir-let rn nm ty val body sp
    is IrLambda (params) (body) (ty) (sp) -> rename-ir-lambda rn params body ty sp
    is IrMatch (scrut) (branches) (ty) (sp) -> IrMatch (rename-ir-expr rn scrut) (rename-ir-branches rn branches 0 []) ty sp
    is IrList (elems) (ty) (sp) -> IrList (for e in elems -> rename-ir-expr rn e) ty sp
    is IrRecord (nm) (fields) (ty) (sp) -> IrRecord nm (for f in fields -> IRFieldVal { name = f.name, value = rename-ir-expr rn (f.value), span = f.span }) ty sp
    is IrAct (stmts) (ty) (sp) -> IrAct (rename-ir-act-stmts rn stmts 0 []) ty sp
    is IrHandle (eff) (body) (clauses) (ty) (sp) -> IrHandle eff (rename-ir-expr rn body) (rename-ir-handle-clauses rn clauses 0 []) ty sp
    is IrWithTimeout (secs) (effs) (scopes) (body) (ty) (sp) -> IrWithTimeout secs effs scopes (rename-ir-expr rn body) ty sp
    is IrTry (max) (body) (fb) (fail) (ty) (sp) -> IrTry max (rename-ir-act-stmts rn body 0 []) (rename-ir-act-stmts rn fb 0 []) (rename-ir-act-stmts rn fail 0 []) ty sp
    is otherwise -> e

  rename-ir-let : List RenameEntry, Text, CodexType, IRExpr, IRExpr, SourceSpan -> IRExpr
  rename-ir-let (rn) (nm) (ty) (val) (body) (sp) =
   let rn2 = remove-rename rn nm
   in IrLet nm ty (rename-ir-expr rn val) (rename-ir-expr rn2 body) sp

  rename-ir-lambda : List RenameEntry, List IRParam, IRExpr, CodexType, SourceSpan -> IRExpr
  rename-ir-lambda (rn) (params) (body) (ty) (sp) =
   let rn2 = remove-renames-for-params rn params 0
   in IrLambda params (rename-ir-expr rn2 body) ty sp

Section: IR Collection Renaming
  rename-ir-branches : List RenameEntry, List IRBranch, Integer, List IRBranch -> List IRBranch
  rename-ir-branches (rn) (branches) (i) (acc) =
   if i == list-length branches then acc
   else let b = list-at branches i
   in let rn2 = remove-renames-for-pat rn (b.pattern)
   in let b2 = deck-record (__record-set b "body" (rename-ir-expr rn2 (b.body)))
   in let b3 = deck-record (__record-set b2 "guard" (rename-ir-expr rn2 (b.guard)))
   in rename-ir-branches rn branches (i + 1) (list-push acc b3)

  remove-renames-for-pat : List RenameEntry, IRPat -> List RenameEntry
  remove-renames-for-pat (rn) (p) =
   when p
    is IrVarPat (nm) (ty) (sp) -> remove-rename rn nm
    is IrCtorPat (nm) (subs) (ty) (sp) -> remove-renames-for-pats rn subs 0
    is IrVecPat (subs) (ty) (sp) -> remove-renames-for-pats rn subs 0
    is IrLitPat (v) (ty) (sp) | IrWildPat (sp) -> rn

  remove-renames-for-pats : List RenameEntry, List IRPat, Integer -> List RenameEntry
  remove-renames-for-pats (rn) (pats) (i) =
   if i == list-length pats then rn
   else remove-renames-for-pats (remove-renames-for-pat rn (list-at pats i)) pats (i + 1)

  rename-ir-act-stmts : List RenameEntry, List IRActStmt, Integer, List IRActStmt -> List IRActStmt
  rename-ir-act-stmts (rn) (stmts) (i) (acc) =
   if i == list-length stmts then acc
   else let stmt = list-at stmts i
   in when stmt
    is IrDoBind (nm) (ty) (expr) (sp) ->
     let rn2 = remove-rename rn nm
   in rename-ir-act-stmts rn2 stmts (i + 1) (list-push acc (IrDoBind nm ty (rename-ir-expr rn expr) sp))
    is IrDoExec (expr) (sp) ->
     rename-ir-act-stmts rn stmts (i + 1) (list-push acc (IrDoExec (rename-ir-expr rn expr) sp))

  rename-ir-handle-clauses : List RenameEntry, List IRHandleClause, Integer, List IRHandleClause -> List IRHandleClause
  rename-ir-handle-clauses (rn) (clauses) (i) (acc) =
   if i == list-length clauses then acc
   else let c = list-at clauses i
   in let rn2 = remove-rename rn (c.resume-name)
   in let rn3 = remove-clause-param-renames rn2 (c.params) 0 (list-length (c.params))
   in rename-ir-handle-clauses rn clauses (i + 1) (list-push acc (IRHandleClause {
    op-name = c.op-name,
    params = c.params,
    resume-name = c.resume-name,
    body = rename-ir-expr rn3 (c.body),
    span = c.span
   }))

  remove-clause-param-renames : List RenameEntry, List Text, Integer, Integer -> List RenameEntry
  remove-clause-param-renames (rn) (ps) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then rn
   else remove-clause-param-renames (remove-rename rn (list-at ps i)) ps (i + 1) len

Section: Chapter Scoping Pipeline
  build-all-assignments : Text, List DefHeader, Integer, List ChapterAssignment -> List ChapterAssignment
  build-all-assignments (source) (headers) (i) (acc) =
   if i == list-length headers then acc
   else let hdr = list-at headers i
   in build-all-assignments source headers (i + 1) (list-push acc (ChapterAssignment {
    def-name = token-text (hdr.name),
    chapter-slug = hdr.chapter-slug
   }))

  build-chapter-rename-map : SkipListText, List ChapterAssignment, Text -> List RenameEntry
  build-chapter-rename-map (colliding) (assignments) (cur-chap) =
   build-mod-renames colliding assignments cur-chap 0 (list-length assignments) []

  build-mod-renames : SkipListText, List ChapterAssignment, Text, Integer, Integer, List RenameEntry -> List RenameEntry
  build-mod-renames (colliding) (assignments) (cur-chap) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else let a = list-at assignments i
   in if is-colliding colliding (a.def-name)
      then if a.chapter-slug == cur-chap
           then let cleaned = remove-rename acc (a.def-name)
   in build-mod-renames colliding assignments cur-chap (i + 1) len (sorted-rename-insert cleaned (RenameEntry {
                 original = a.def-name,
                 mangled = mangle-name cur-chap (a.def-name)
                }))
           else if has-rename acc (a.def-name)
                then build-mod-renames colliding assignments cur-chap (i + 1) len acc
                else build-mod-renames colliding assignments cur-chap (i + 1) len (sorted-rename-insert acc (RenameEntry {
                 original = a.def-name,
                 mangled = mangle-name (a.chapter-slug) (a.def-name)
                }))
      else build-mod-renames colliding assignments cur-chap (i + 1) len acc

  build-global-rename-table : List ChapterAssignment, SkipListText -> List RenameEntry
  build-global-rename-table (assignments) (colliding) =
   let pushed = build-global-renames assignments colliding (list-length assignments - 1) (__list-with-capacity (list-length assignments))
   in dedupe-renames (sort-by pushed compare-rename-originals)

 The table is built by a single reversed push pass, one merge sort,
 and an adjacent dedupe - O(n log n). The previous per-entry
 sorted-rename-insert rebuilt the whole immutable list on every
 insert: O(n squared) ALLOCATION, and because the call site runs
 inside deck-record the quadratic garbage landed on the scope deck
 and was retained for the entire compile (~180 MB on the selfhost -
 the largest single retained block in the compiler, found by the
 MEASURE deck-span map). Winner preservation: the old leftmost-equal
 bsearch returned the LAST-inserted duplicate; pushing in reverse
 assignment order and relying on merge-sort stability puts that same
 entry leftmost, and the dedupe keeps it.

  compare-rename-originals : RenameEntry, RenameEntry -> Integer
  compare-rename-originals (a) (b) = text-compare (a.original) (b.original)

  build-global-renames : List ChapterAssignment, SkipListText, Integer, List RenameEntry -> List RenameEntry
  build-global-renames (assignments) (colliding) (i) (acc) =
   if i < 0 then acc
   else let a = list-at assignments i
   in if is-colliding colliding (a.def-name)
      then build-global-renames assignments colliding (i - 1) (list-push acc (RenameEntry {
            original = a.def-name,
            mangled = mangle-name (a.chapter-slug) (a.def-name)
           }))
      else build-global-renames assignments colliding (i - 1) acc

  dedupe-renames : List RenameEntry -> List RenameEntry
  dedupe-renames (entries) =
   dedupe-renames-loop entries 0 (list-length entries) (__list-with-capacity (list-length entries))

  dedupe-renames-loop : List RenameEntry, Integer, Integer, List RenameEntry -> List RenameEntry
  dedupe-renames-loop (entries) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else let e = list-at entries i
   in if i > 0
      then if (list-at entries (i - 1)).original == e.original
           then dedupe-renames-loop entries (i + 1) len acc
           else dedupe-renames-loop entries (i + 1) len (list-push acc e)
      else dedupe-renames-loop entries (i + 1) len (list-push acc e)

  resolve-def-name : List RenameEntry, Text, Text -> Text
  resolve-def-name (renames) (name) (chapter-slug) =
   if rename-has-entry renames name then mangle-name chapter-slug name
   else name

  has-rename : List RenameEntry, Text -> Boolean
  has-rename (entries) (name) =
   rename-has-entry entries name

Section: Duplicate Cite Detection
  cite-selects-name : ACitesDecl, Text -> Boolean
  cite-selects-name (c) (name) =
   cite-selects-name-loop (c.selected-names) name 0 (list-length (c.selected-names))

  cite-selects-name-loop : List Name, Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  cite-selects-name-loop (names) (target) (j) (len) =
   if j == len then False
   else if (list-at names j).value == target then True
   else cite-selects-name-loop names target (j + 1) len

  find-cite-conflict : List ACitesDecl, Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  find-cite-conflict (citations) (citing) (name) (k) (total) =
   if k == total then ""
   else let c = list-at citations k
   in if c.citing-chapter == citing then if cite-selects-name c name then c.chapter-name.value
     else find-cite-conflict citations citing name (k + 1) total
     else find-cite-conflict citations citing name (k + 1) total

  add-duplicate-cite-error : DiagnosticBag, ACitesDecl, Text, Text -> DiagnosticBag
  add-duplicate-cite-error (bag) (cite) (name-val) (conflict) =
   bag-add bag (make-error cdx-duplicate-cite ("Name '" & name-val & "' is cited from both '" & (cite.chapter-name.value) & "' and '" & conflict & "' in chapter '" & (cite.citing-chapter) & "'") (cite.span))

  check-cite-names : ACitesDecl, Integer, Integer, List ACitesDecl, Integer, Integer, DiagnosticBag -> DiagnosticBag
  check-cite-names (cite) (j) (nlen) (all-citations) (i) (total) (bag) =
   if j == nlen then bag
   else let name = list-at (cite.selected-names) j
   in let conflict = find-cite-conflict all-citations (cite.citing-chapter) (name.value) (i + 1) total
   in let bag2 = if conflict == "" then bag else add-duplicate-cite-error bag cite (name.value) conflict
   in check-cite-names cite (j + 1) nlen all-citations i total bag2

  check-duplicate-cites-loop : List ACitesDecl, Integer, Integer, DiagnosticBag -> DiagnosticBag
  check-duplicate-cites-loop (citations) (i) (len) (bag) =
   if i == len then bag
   else let cite-i = list-at citations i
   in let bag2 = check-cite-names cite-i 0 (list-length (cite-i.selected-names)) citations i len bag
   in check-duplicate-cites-loop citations (i + 1) len bag2

  check-duplicate-cites : List ACitesDecl -> DiagnosticBag
  check-duplicate-cites (citations) =
   check-duplicate-cites-loop citations 0 (list-length citations) empty-bag

 A cite that names no chapter in the unit was silent: it type-checked,
 compiled and ran with no diagnostic, and the guarantee lived entirely in
 the build resolver's PowerShell. A second front end would not inherit it.

 It is an ERROR (CDX3007) since 2026-07-20, and both the warning period
 and the flip were decided by measurement rather than by taste. It began
 as a warning because as an error it would have halted the sign tool and
 three sibling tools -- all citing Foreword chapter Console, all
 assembled without Console.codex, a class the presence check could not
 see while it asked only the per-definition assignments (Console
 declares only effect operations). The header-presence fix
 resolved that whole class, and the warning then measured ZERO
 firings across every population anyone could assemble: all 265 app
 entry units, all 1,154 test units compiled standalone, the selfhost,
 the sign tool, the canary and the plug bundles. A cite that names no
 chapter in the unit is a broken unit, the host resolver already
 refuses the same fact as CDX3010, and with the false-positive class
 gone there is nothing left for a warning to measure.

 Two normalizations have to be right here, and getting either wrong makes
 the check reject correct code. Both were measured against the real tree
 before this was written, not reasoned about.

 FIRST: match on the BARE chapter name, not on <Quire>--<Chapter>. The
 concat prefixes each header with its DIRECTORY, and a quire is not a
 directory -- everything under codex/compiler is the Codex quire but sits in
 Core, Emit, Types and the rest. So `cites Codex chapter Build Settings`
 has to match the chapter the concat named `Core--Build Settings`. Composing
 the quire into the key misses 21 of the compiler's own 73 cites; the bare
 key misses none.

 SECOND: the key DROPS spaces, it does not turn them into hyphens, so
 slugify is the wrong function to reach for. A cite may name the FILE while
 the header names the chapter: `cites Common chapter ByteHelpers` is
 satisfied by `ByteHelpers.codex`, whose header reads `Chapter: Byte
 Helpers`. Neither form is wrong and no key built on one finds the other
 unless the spaces come out. This is the build resolver's Get-CiteKey rule
 (`build/quire-map.ps1`), and it is deliberately the same rule -- a second
 notion of matching is how the two ends drift apart. Using slugify here
 rejected both of the pe writers' correct cites.

 find-slug-for-cite-name used to compose the key the first way and so
 answered "" for those same 21 cites, dropping their cite overrides. It
 now tries the composed key first and falls back to the bare name, which
 is why both spellings keep working.

 The consequence of that "" was sharper than a dropped override, and
 codex/test/cite-override-quire.codex pins it: if the cited name is
 CONTESTED, both definitions are mangled, the citing chapter gets no
 rename entry, and the mention resolves to nothing -- CDX3002, no binary.
 So the old key did not quietly choose the wrong definition, it made a
 whole construct uncompilable. It went unnoticed because every cite
 written the quire way happens to name an uncontested definition.

 The fallback REFUSES rather than guesses. A bare name can be ambiguous
 where <Quire>--<Chapter> was not: ten of them are, measured over the real
 source -- Console lives in both core and kernel, Opening in both compiler
 and test, and Markdown in both encode and ui. Matching bare and taking the
 first hit would mangle a name to whichever chapter the concat happened to
 emit first, which is a wrong answer that compiles. So when the bare name
 matches more than one chapter in the unit the answer is "" -- exactly what
 the broken key gave, no worse than before, and never a guess. Note this is
 not the same question the presence check asks: a duplicate bare name is
 still PRESENT, so it warrants no CDX3007.

 This walks the citations ONCE. apply-cite-overrides-loop runs per chapter
 and would report every miss as many times as there are chapters.

  chapter-slug-dashes : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  chapter-slug-dashes (s) (i) (len) =
   if i + 1 >= len then -1
   else if char-code-at s i == char-code-at "-" 0 then if char-code-at s (i + 1) == char-code-at "-" 0 then i
     else chapter-slug-dashes s (i + 1) len
     else chapter-slug-dashes s (i + 1) len

  chapter-bare-name : Text -> Text
  chapter-bare-name (s) =
   let p = chapter-slug-dashes s 0 (text-length s)
   in if p < 0 then s else substring s (p + 2) (text-length s - p - 2)

  cite-key-loop : Text, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  cite-key-loop (s) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else let c = char-code-at s i
   in if c == char-code-at " " 0 then cite-key-loop s (i + 1) len acc
      else if c >= cc-first-upper then if c <= cc-upper-z then cite-key-loop s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (char-to-text (code-to-char (c - 26))))
      else cite-key-loop s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (char-to-text (code-to-char c)))
      else cite-key-loop s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (char-to-text (code-to-char c)))

  cite-key : Text -> Text
  cite-key (s) = text-concat-list (cite-key-loop s 0 (text-length s) [])

  add-missing-cite-error : DiagnosticBag, ACitesDecl -> DiagnosticBag
  add-missing-cite-error (bag) (cite) =
   bag-add bag (make-error cdx-cite-not-in-unit ("Cited chapter '" & (cite.chapter-name.value) & "' of quire '" & (cite.quire.value) & "' is not present in this compilation unit") (cite.span))

  bare-name-present : List Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  bare-name-present (bare-cache) (target) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then False
   else if list-at bare-cache i == target then True
   else bare-name-present bare-cache target (i + 1) len

  check-cites-resolve-loop : List ACitesDecl, List Text, Integer, Integer, DiagnosticBag -> DiagnosticBag
  check-cites-resolve-loop (citations) (bare-cache) (i) (len) (bag) =
   if i == len then bag
   else let cite = list-at citations i
   in if bare-name-present bare-cache (cite-key (cite.chapter-name.value)) 0 (list-length bare-cache)
      then check-cites-resolve-loop citations bare-cache (i + 1) len bag
      else check-cites-resolve-loop citations bare-cache (i + 1) len (add-missing-cite-error bag cite)

 Presence is asked of the chapter headers the scan passed, not only of
 the chapters that own a definition. ChapterAssignment is built per
 definition, so a chapter declaring only an effect or a type contributed
 no assignment and read as absent though it sat in the unit. Both
 sources feed the cache: the assignments still answer for a bundled
 chapter renamed Quire--Name, whose header the scan records under the
 prefixed form and whose bare name chapter-bare-name recovers.

  check-cites-resolve : List ACitesDecl, List ChapterAssignment, List Text -> DiagnosticBag
  check-cites-resolve (citations) (assignments) (chapter-names) =
   let from-defs = for a in assignments -> cite-key (chapter-bare-name (a.chapter-slug))
   in let from-headers = for n in chapter-names -> cite-key (chapter-bare-name n)
   in check-cites-resolve-loop citations (deck-record (from-defs & from-headers)) 0 (list-length citations) empty-bag

Section: AST-Level Chapter Scoping
  scope-achapter : AChapter, SkipListText, List ChapterAssignment, Integer -> AChapter
  scope-achapter (ast) (colliding) (assignments) (ceiling) =
   let slug-cache = for a in assignments -> cite-key (a.chapter-slug)
   in let raw-defs = scope-adefs-ll (ast.defs) colliding assignments (ast.citations) slug-cache "" [] 0 [] ceiling
   in deck-record (AChapter {
    name = ast.name,
    defs = deck-record (__linked-list-to-list raw-defs),
    type-defs = ast.type-defs,
    effect-defs = ast.effect-defs,
    class-defs = ast.class-defs,
    instance-defs = ast.instance-defs,
    citations = ast.citations,
    ground-effects = ast.ground-effects,
    chapter-title = ast.chapter-title,
    prose = ast.prose,
    prose-blocks = ast.prose-blocks,
    annotations = ast.annotations,
    section-titles = ast.section-titles,
    rt-names = ast.rt-names,
    rt-budgets = ast.rt-budgets,
    conversions = ast.conversions,
    span = ast.span
   })

 The ceiling is the SCOPE deck reservation's top, and it is checked here
 because this loop is the only place the phase writes the deck in bulk:
 one renamed ADef per definition. Past the ceiling the deck grows into
 the bivy and the phase reads its own overwrite. A phase
 that stops here leaves the deck short of its ceiling, which is what
 compile-desugar-and-scope tests to raise CDX9002.

  scope-adefs-ll : List ADef, SkipListText, List ChapterAssignment, List ACitesDecl, List Text, Text, List RenameEntry, Integer, LinkedList ADef, Integer -> LinkedList ADef
  scope-adefs-ll (defs) (colliding) (assignments) (citations) (slug-cache) (cur-slug) (cur-rn) (i) (acc) (ceiling) =
   if i == list-length defs then acc
   else if deck-short-of ceiling demand-scope-guard-band then acc
   else let d = list-at defs i
   in let rn = if d.chapter-slug == cur-slug then cur-rn
      else apply-cite-overrides (build-chapter-rename-map colliding assignments (d.chapter-slug)) citations colliding assignments slug-cache (d.chapter-slug) 0
   in let new-name = deck-record (Name { value = rename-lookup rn (d.name.value) })
   in scope-adefs-ll defs colliding assignments citations slug-cache (d.chapter-slug) rn (i + 1) (__linked-list-push acc (deck-record (ADef {
    name = new-name,
    params = d.params,
    declared-type = d.declared-type,
    body = d.body,
    chapter-slug = d.chapter-slug,
    span = d.span
   }))) ceiling

  apply-cite-overrides : List RenameEntry, List ACitesDecl, SkipListText, List ChapterAssignment, List Text, Text, Integer -> List RenameEntry
  apply-cite-overrides (rn) (citations) (colliding) (assignments) (slug-cache) (cur-slug) (i) =
   apply-cite-overrides-loop rn citations colliding assignments slug-cache cur-slug i

  apply-cite-overrides-loop : List RenameEntry, List ACitesDecl, SkipListText, List ChapterAssignment, List Text, Text, Integer -> List RenameEntry
  apply-cite-overrides-loop (rn) (citations) (colliding) (assignments) (slug-cache) (cur-slug) (i) =
   if i == list-length citations then rn
   else let cite = list-at citations i
   in let cite-slug = find-slug-for-cite-name (cite.quire.value) (cite.chapter-name.value) assignments slug-cache
   in let rn2 = apply-cite-selected rn (cite.selected-names) cite-slug colliding assignments cur-slug 0
   in apply-cite-overrides-loop rn2 citations colliding assignments slug-cache cur-slug (i + 1)

 The suffix test compares characters in place. It runs per assignment,
 per citation that misses the composed key, per slug transition of the
 defs walk -- and the walk runs in all four passes, one of which (LOWER)
 retains every byte it allocates until the phase compact. Written with
 `substring`, this one comparison put 5.4 MB per bare-name citation on
 the LOWER deck, about 135 MB of the selfhost's 302.

  slug-has-suffix : Text, Text -> Boolean
  slug-has-suffix (s) (suf) =
   let sl = text-length s
   in let fl = text-length suf
   in if fl > sl then False else slug-suffix-eq s suf (sl - fl) 0 fl

  slug-suffix-eq : Text, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  slug-suffix-eq (s) (suf) (base) (j) (fl) =
   if j == fl then True
   else if char-code-at s (base + j) == char-code-at suf j then slug-suffix-eq s suf base (j + 1) fl
   else False

  slug-matches-bare : Text, Text, Text -> Boolean
  slug-matches-bare (s) (bare) (suf) =
   if s == bare then True else slug-has-suffix s suf

  count-bare-matches : Text, Text, List Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  count-bare-matches (bare) (suf) (slug-cache) (i) (len) (n) =
   if i == len then n
   else if slug-matches-bare (list-at slug-cache i) bare suf then count-bare-matches bare suf slug-cache (i + 1) len (n + 1)
      else count-bare-matches bare suf slug-cache (i + 1) len n

  find-slug-bare-loop : Text, Text, List ChapterAssignment, List Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  find-slug-bare-loop (bare) (suf) (assignments) (slug-cache) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then ""
   else if slug-matches-bare (list-at slug-cache i) bare suf then (list-at assignments i).chapter-slug
      else find-slug-bare-loop bare suf assignments slug-cache (i + 1) len

  find-slug-by-bare-name : Text, List ChapterAssignment, List Text -> Text
  find-slug-by-bare-name (bare) (assignments) (slug-cache) =
   let suf = "--" & bare
   in if count-bare-matches bare suf slug-cache 0 (list-length slug-cache) 0 == 1
      then find-slug-bare-loop bare suf assignments slug-cache 0 (list-length assignments)
      else ""

  find-slug-for-cite-name : Text, Text, List ChapterAssignment, List Text -> Text
  find-slug-for-cite-name (quire) (cite-name) (assignments) (slug-cache) =
   let exact = find-slug-for-cite-name-loop (cite-key (quire & "--" & cite-name)) assignments slug-cache 0 (list-length assignments)
   in if exact == "" then find-slug-by-bare-name (cite-key cite-name) assignments slug-cache
      else exact

  find-slug-for-cite-name-loop : Text, List ChapterAssignment, List Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  find-slug-for-cite-name-loop (slug-cite) (assignments) (slug-cache) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then ""
   else if list-at slug-cache i == slug-cite then (list-at assignments i).chapter-slug
      else find-slug-for-cite-name-loop slug-cite assignments slug-cache (i + 1) len

  apply-cite-selected : List RenameEntry, List Name, Text, SkipListText, List ChapterAssignment, Text, Integer -> List RenameEntry
  apply-cite-selected (rn) (names) (cite-slug) (colliding) (assignments) (cur-slug) (i) =
   if i == list-length names then rn
   else let n = (list-at names i).value
   in if is-colliding colliding n then if chapter-defines-name assignments cur-slug n
     then apply-cite-selected rn names cite-slug colliding assignments cur-slug (i + 1)
   else let cleaned = remove-rename rn n
   in apply-cite-selected (sorted-rename-insert cleaned (RenameEntry {
      original = n,
      mangled = mangle-name cite-slug n
     })) names cite-slug colliding assignments cur-slug (i + 1)
     else apply-cite-selected rn names cite-slug colliding assignments cur-slug (i + 1)

  chapter-defines-name : List ChapterAssignment, Text, Text -> Boolean
  chapter-defines-name (assignments) (slug) (name) =
   chapter-defines-loop assignments slug name 0 (list-length assignments)

  chapter-defines-loop : List ChapterAssignment, Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  chapter-defines-loop (assignments) (slug) (name) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then False
   else let a = list-at assignments i
   in if a.def-name == name then if a.chapter-slug == slug then True
      else chapter-defines-loop assignments slug name (i + 1) len
      else chapter-defines-loop assignments slug name (i + 1) len

